84. Ne3
{ The move that completely stabilizes! Knight to e3 - by no means does Black fall apart, you'll still need 100 more moves to win, but it's a step in the right direction. Now you can't just bring the queen back and forth - the knight on e3 guards c4 and c2, and Black has no way of getting to the white king. For example Bd3, you can go Qd1, and even if Be4, still no way to get anything done. King to b3, this is guarded, and you will slowly but surely get the queen into the game and eventually win. }
( 84. Qd2
{ After Qd3, Qd2 was played offering a queen trade. Ian just kept repeating moves. }
84... Qf1
{ Queen to f1 - and it was in this position on move 84 that the game ended in threefold repetition (draw agreements are not allowed in the Grand Chess Tour, so it had to be threefold repetition). An incredible game - in the best game of the tournament in terms of spectator pleasure. The Marshall had everything, so many lines could have gone wrong for either player, but it was almost played to perfection. Gukesh showed incredible preparation; Ian was a little outprepared but managed to find his way and force a draw in a winning position. }
